WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

1
00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:22.490
Inside the womb, the unborn baby is surrounded by gentle and calming sounds, including their

2
00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:31.870
mother's heartbeat and digestive system and voices and sounds from the world outside. As

3
00:00:31.870 --> 00:00:38.750
pregnancy reaches its final stages, the baby's eyes can open inside the womb. While they can't

4
00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:46.950
yet see images or details, they can sense changes in light. So, while life in the womb is muffled

5
00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:56.840
and dim, it is far from silent. Birth is a huge change for a baby. They suddenly enter

6
00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:03.820
a world full of new, bright sights and loud sounds. Adjusting to this new world is a sensory

7
00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:10.340
awakening that kick-starts their development. In the first few weeks of life, the baby will

8
00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:17.200
startle and wake up if there are loud sounds. Their eyes are sensitive to bright lights,

9
00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:25.330
but they can still see very little. Their vision is, however, starting to develop at a staggering

10
00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:33.240
rate. Within two weeks, vision has improved hugely. They can now see some detail, especially

11
00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:39.780
if things are up close. There is no colour, and what they see is flat because their eyes

12
00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:45.880
are not yet always straight and working together for depth perception. Sometimes their eyes

13
00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:52.730
will drift in or out for a short while. This is called squinting. This should stop happening

14
00:01:52.730 --> 00:02:00.340
by three months. By one month, colour vision is starting to develop. They will be much

15
00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:06.160
more visually interested, especially to faces. They will also start to try to follow things

16
00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:13.030
that are moving. They will become more and more aware of familiar sounds, including well-known

17
00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:19.450
voices, like their family, and start to join up what they hear with what they

18
00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:24.820
see. By three months, their eyes should always be straight and working together to create

19
00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:31.620
3D vision. This new depth perception helps them reach for things more accurately. They

20
00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:37.240
will be able to follow things that are moving faster. At the same time, meaningful sounds

21
00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:45.420
will start to be made. By six months, the baby should be able to recognise familiar people

22
00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:50.960
by both their faces and voices. They can also start to see things that are further

23
00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:57.880
away. Driven by curiosity, they will reach for things, crawl towards them, and, around

24
00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:03.760
their first birthday, walk. Their babblings are developing and sounds will become first

25
00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:13.460
words at this stage as well. By 18 months, vision is quite adult-like, although still

26
00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:20.520
a bit blurry. They will now be able to recognise their name and be using more and more words.

27
00:03:22.070 --> 00:03:28.300
Some babies may not achieve these developmental milestones at the expected time. There are

28
00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:34.210
many reasons for this, and many will correct themselves. For example, premature babies

29
00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:40.920
may have delays, but typically catch up later in childhood. For some, there may be persisting

30
00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:47.710
difficulties. Identifying these as early as possible to offer treatment and support can

31
00:03:47.710 --> 00:03:49.210
maximise development.

